{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This is the statute of the Torah which HaShem commanded, saying.</b> Twice 'saying' — why do I need it, for it has already been stated, ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְאֶל אַהֲרֹן לֵאמֹר״? And it appears that it is because the nations taunt Israel specifically over this commandment, whose reason is hidden from the eye of all living, and they say, 'What is this service to you?' And the Torah says, ״עֲנֵה כְסִיל כְּאִוַּלְתּוֹ פֶּן יִהְיֶה חָכָם בְּעֵינָיו״ — answer a fool according to his folly, lest he be wise in his own eyes — and he will cast aspersion on the whole Torah. And this is what it says: ״אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ לֵאמֹר״ — that you must say and respond to the hasty scoffer that this commandment is 'the statute of the Torah,' meaning the decree of the King, blessed be His name, upon us, and we have no permission to harbor doubts about it, just as Rashi explained the answer to us — so too according to this explanation shall the formulation of this answer be directed to the scoffing scoffer. Therefore it says ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״ and not ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַפָּרָה״, because this formulation is a response to the sectarians who taunt Israel, to say to them: it is the decree of the King, blessed be He, for 'the Torah' stands in place of 'the King,' since from Him, blessed be He, Torah goes forth — so that they not say that this commandment is not among the commandments of the Torah." "And as for what is written in parashat Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 4:6), ״כִּי הִיא חָכְמַתְכֶם וּבִינַתְכֶם לְעֵינֵי הָעַמִּים אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁמְעוּן אֵת כָּל הַחֻקִּים הָאֵלֶּה״ — while here it implies, on the contrary, that they taunt over the statutes — the answer to this is that there are statutes whose reason human intellect can grasp, besides those whose reason is hidden from the multitudes yet revealed to the singular survivors of the generation; and this parah — its reason is hidden from every person, for Solomon attained all except the parah, about which he said ״וְהִיא רְחוֹקָה מִמֶּנִּי״ (Ecclesiastes 7:23). Therefore the nations who hear the statute of the parah alone certainly taunt over it. But there it says ״אֵת כָּל הַחֻקִּים הָאֵלֶּה״ — for those who hear all the statutes together, and among them are statutes that have a revealed reason — those same nations will testify and declare even about the hidden statute that it contains a secret, and they will attribute the deficiency to the limited understanding of the one who grasps it." "<b>And they shall take to you a cow, etc.</b> Rashi explained: it is always called by your name, etc. What honor and greatness did it give to Moses that the cow should be called by his name? And it seems close to me to say about this that a commandment is called only after the one who completes it, and according to our Rabbis of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 19:8) the parah atones for the sin of the Calf — let the mother come and wipe away the filth of her son. And just as Moses began this atonement when he burned the Calf and ground it fine to dust, so too he would complete the atonement, and the completion as well would be called by his name. And this is the reason our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yalkut Shimoni, Yitro, remez 268): when Moses heard the Holy One, blessed be He, state a halakhah in the name of Eliezer — 'a parah in its second year, an eglah in its first year' — he said: May it be the will that this Eliezer be among those who issue from my loins, as it says, ״וְשֵׁם הָאֶחָד אֱלִיעֶזֶר״ (Exodus 18:4). And what did Moses see to request concerning this particular law, that it issue from those who issue from his loins, more than all the hidden mysteries of the Torah that are stated in truth in every generation, and those who seek them generation after generation, and their sages? Rather, it is because in the matter of the Calf Moses delivered his soul over Israel, as it says ״מְחֵנִי נָא מִסִּפְרֶךָ״ (Exodus 32:32), and they were called by his name, as it says (Isaiah 63:11) ״וַיִּזְכֹּר יְמֵי עוֹלָם מֹשֶׁה עַמּוֹ״. Therefore he wanted that also in this parah, from beginning to end, it should be called by his name, for the parah's reason is to uproot the root of idolatry; and so too the eglah arufah atones for the shedding of blood, for in the matter of the Calf they also transgressed the shedding of blood, in the killing of Hur. Therefore it says ״וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ״ — that the completion of this commandment shall be called by your name." "And since it says ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״ and not ״חֻקַּת הַפָּרָה״, infer from it that the Torah and the parah are one matter: just as the Torah is called by Moses' name, as it says (Malachi 3:22) ״זִכְרוּ תּוֹרַת מֹשֶׁה עַבְדִּי״, so too the parah is called by his name. And the reason for both of them is one, for fifty gates of understanding were created in the world and all of them were given to Moses lacking one (Nedarim 38a), and by means of the forty-nine gates of understanding that he attained he had an entrance to enter into the secret of HaShem, to expound the Torah in forty-nine faces pure and in forty-nine faces impure, as it says (Proverbs 2:4) ״וְכַמַּטְמוֹנִים תַּחְפְּשֶׂנָּה״ — 'hidden treasures' — counting by twice forty-nine, for by means of this the אִמְרוֹת of HaShem are אִמְרוֹת טְהוֹרוֹת מְזֻקָּקוֹת שִׁבְעָתָיִם (Psalms 12:7), that is, seven upon seven. And so too the reason of the parah is hidden from the eye of all living, even from Solomon, about whom it says ״וַיֶּחְכַּם מִכָּל הָאָדָם״ (1 Kings 5:11), and it was not revealed except to Moses, by means of the forty-nine gates of understanding that he attained, as is concluded in Yalkut Shimoni (Psalms 12, remez 648): ״כֶּסֶף צָרוּף בַּעֲלִיל לָאָרֶץ מְזֻקָּק שִׁבְעָתָיִם״ — Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi interpreted the verse regarding parashat parah, that it contains seven times seven: seven cows, seven sprinklings, seven burnings, seven washings, seven impure persons, seven pure persons, seven priests. If a person says to you, they are short, say to him: even Moses and Aaron were included in the count. Thus far its language. And one may inquire: what practical inference do we have from this number, that he expounded a verse upon it, ״מְזֻקָּק שִׁבְעָתָיִם״? But surely the intent of the author of this midrash is to give a reason for what is stated in the midrash (Yalkut Shimoni, Chukat 19, remez 759): ״וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ פָרָה״ — 'To you I reveal the secret of the parah, but to others it is a חֻקָּה.' And why was it hidden from the eye of all living? Because it contains the number forty-nine, and this is a hint that one cannot refine and combine all of its matter except by means of the forty-nine gates of understanding that were not revealed except to Moses. Therefore it is to others a חֻקָּה, even to Solomon." "And in the Akeidah he cited the midrash that says that the parah was called 'Bat Sheva,' the mother of Solomon, and he explained accordingly what he explained. And I say: just as the mother rules over the son, so too the parah ruled over all the wisdoms of Solomon, for he was unable to enter into her secret. And through this is settled why ״חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״ is said only here and in parashat Matot, regarding the laws of vessels absorbed through a menstruant's waters — because the purification of the parah in its totality is likened to the Torah in its totality, by reason of the hidden mysteries of wisdom contained within it." "<b>A red heifer, without blemish.</b> A parable: to the child of a maidservant who soiled the king's palace — they said, let the mother come and wipe up her child's filth (Tanhuma Ḥukkat 8). For whoever wishes to nullify some evil growth, it does not suffice to lop off the branches; as long as its stock is in the earth, its root will not die — putting forth shoot and wormwood — for it will ultimately grow back and sprout anew. But with the uprooting of the root, the branches too fall, as it is written (Amos 2:9): ״וָאַשְׁמִיד פִּרְיוֹ מִמַּעַל וְשָׁרָשָׁיו מִתָּחַת״ — hear from this that destroying the fruit alone is not enough. Therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avodah Zarah 45a): one who uproots idolatry must search out its root after it — that is to say, one must investigate what cause led them to worship idolatry, for the cause is the root putting forth shoot and wormwood, and with the nullification of the cause, the consequent falls — that is, the offspring. And the cause is likened to the mother that gives birth to the offspring; therefore HaShem commanded the burning of the cow, the mother of the calf, so that we may learn from this the law that one who uproots idolatry must search out its root, and then they will not return to their former folly. And in parashat Ki Tissa (Exodus 30:13) we spoke at length on this matter, and there it is explained that the principal cause was the abundance of silver and gold that the Holy One, blessed be He, bestowed upon them, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 32a): 'You caused them' etc. And because this red, red gold brought them to such a deed, therefore they shall take a red heifer to burn and nullify this red appearance — 'and gold will not multiply for them anymore' — and then they will not come to such a deed again." "And this profound matter, and the parable and its resolution, HaShem showed through the deed of the calf — for it caused the impurity of the dead. For at the giving of the Torah, Israel became free of the tablets — free from the Angel of Death (Shemot Rabbah 41:7) — and the meaning is not that the intention was to abolish death entirely, but rather this freedom was specifically from the Angel of Death, that they would die only by a divine kiss, and then the dead would not be impure. For the entire essence of the impurity of the dead comes from the death that is brought about by the Angel of Death, who comes from the side of impurity; but those who die by a kiss have no aspect of impurity whatsoever — for on the day that our holy Rabbi died, they said that on that very day the priesthood was annulled (Ketubot 103a, cf. Tosafot ad loc.), for there was no impurity there; therefore they permitted the priests to attend to him. It follows that at the receiving of the Torah they became free men, liberated from the Angel of Death, and impurity was annulled. And through the calf that they made, they returned to their corruption and again subjected themselves to the Angel of Death. If so, the calf caused the impurity of the dead; therefore let the mother come and wipe up her child's filth, to remove the impurity through the ashes of the cow. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "The acronym ילק״ש is resolved as Yalkut Shimoni; the verse citation from Isaiah 63:11 is supplied by the text in parentheses. The phrase 'כַּפָּרָה עַל' is read as 'atones for,' following the idiom established by the rabbinic dictum quoted immediately after.", 3 "The Yalkut Shimoni remez numbers as printed (תרנח and תשנט) are given in Hebrew letter-numerals; they are rendered here in their standard Arabic-numeral equivalents. The phrase 'כִּי עַל יְדֵי זֶה אִמְרוֹת ה׳' uses the wordplay: אִמְרוֹת ('sayings/words') is homiletically linked to אֱמֶת ('truth') and the root of 'hidden treasures' (מַטְמוֹנִים), counted at twice forty-nine; the English preserves the Hebrew terms where the wordplay depends on them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:34.682817918Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}